WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.161
Welcome to Lecture 3 of the

2
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.780
FrontierCS 2 course, on architecture.

3
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.662
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:09.490
removes its key idea from

5
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.107
the authors' own code

6
00:00:11.107 --> 00:00:13.850
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.516
This lecture covers three tasks

8
00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:19.629
a branch predictor, a GPU matrix multiply

9
00:00:19.629 --> 00:00:21.800
and a sparse matrix multiply.

10
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.830
We start with branches.

11
00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.682
A processor fetches instructions far

12
00:00:26.745 --> 00:00:28.890
ahead of the ones it is executing.

13
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.752
At a conditional branch

14
00:00:30.752 --> 00:00:33.208
it cannot wait to learn the direction

15
00:00:33.208 --> 00:00:35.160
so it predicts it and goes on.

16
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:37.224
A wrong guess throws away

17
00:00:37.293 --> 00:00:38.870
the work fetched since.

18
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.209
The task counts both costs per

19
00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:43.794
thousand instructions: mispredictions

20
00:00:43.794 --> 00:00:46.180
and cycles spent on the wrong path.

21
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.872
The simplest predictor keeps one

22
00:00:48.938 --> 00:00:50.720
two-bit counter per branch

23
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.040
from zero to three.

24
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:54.632
It predicts taken at two or three

25
00:00:54.632 --> 00:00:57.270
and moves one step toward each outcome.

26
00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.421
Take the exit test of a loop

27
00:00:59.485 --> 00:01:00.900
that runs eight times.

28
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.272
Over eight hundred branches

29
00:01:03.272 --> 00:01:06.450
the counter misses one hundred: every exit.

30
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.633
On a branch that alternates

31
00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:11.120
it misses four hundred, half of them.

32
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.234
A predictor that remembers

33
00:01:13.293 --> 00:01:14.592
history can do better.

34
00:01:14.651 --> 00:01:16.834
It keeps one counter for each pattern

35
00:01:16.893 --> 00:01:18.900
of the branch's last few outcomes.

36
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:20.444
Pause and predict.

37
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.698
How many outcomes of history does

38
00:01:22.765 --> 00:01:24.290
the loop of eight need?

39
00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:28.590
Seven.

40
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:30.532
One outcome is enough for

41
00:01:30.596 --> 00:01:32.060
the alternating branch.

42
00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.733
But only after seven takens in a

43
00:01:34.805 --> 00:01:37.926
row is the loop's exit next, so with seven

44
00:01:37.926 --> 00:01:39.450
it misses just once.

45
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.619
Real predictors such as TAGE

46
00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:42.983
keep several tables

47
00:01:43.048 --> 00:01:44.673
with histories from a few

48
00:01:44.738 --> 00:01:46.167
branches to thousands

49
00:01:46.232 --> 00:01:48.051
and use the longest one that

50
00:01:48.116 --> 00:01:49.480
has seen the pattern.

51
00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.893
A statistical corrector then adds

52
00:01:51.955 --> 00:01:53.830
votes from other small tables.

53
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.410
Some branches follow data.

54
00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.750
Our toy branch tests whether a loaded value

55
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.517
from zero to sixty-three

56
00:02:01.517 --> 00:02:03.080
is divisible by three.

57
00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.070
To any history, that looks random.

58
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.503
But if the load has already run

59
00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:10.976
the value itself predicts the branch

60
00:02:11.041 --> 00:02:12.733
and in our toy it is ready

61
00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.100
three times in four.

62
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.112
Over a thousand branches

63
00:02:16.112 --> 00:02:18.770
history misses four hundred and twenty.

64
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.567
With the register value

65
00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:23.280
misses fall to one hundred and thirty-seven.

66
00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:25.869
This is the idea of RUNLTS

67
00:02:25.952 --> 00:02:28.854
the winner of the 2025 Championship

68
00:02:28.937 --> 00:02:30.430
Branch Prediction.

69
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.890
So: a counter learns a bias.

70
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.000
History learns a pattern.

71
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.430
And some branches follow data.

72
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:39.803
As an exercise

73
00:02:39.803 --> 00:02:42.735
make the value ready only one time in two

74
00:02:42.735 --> 00:02:44.780
and predict the misses first.

75
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.650
Next, the RUNLTS task itself.
